        "original_id": "b39bed52-cf15-5413-bf4e-e4e1b5d08efd",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Message to Love\n- People: Kris Kristofferson, Rikki Farr\n- Events: Isle of Wight Festival 1970\n- Topics: festival disorder, songs\n\nFacts:\n- The main program of the 1970 Isle of Wight Festival was held on August 26–30, 1970.\n- The film Message to Love depicts the 1970 Isle of Wight Festival as chaotic, showing gate-crashing, crowd incursions onto the stage, Kris Kristofferson being booed offstage, and promoter Rikki Farr ranting at the audience.\n- Kris Kristofferson was born on June 22, 1936.\n- Kris Kristofferson is an American singer-songwriter, musician, and actor.\n- Kris Kristofferson wrote and recorded \"Me and Bobby McGee,\" \"For the Good Times,\" \"Sunday Mornin' Comin' Down,\" and \"Help Me Make It Through the Night.\"",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a8c158d8-25bc-569c-8134-f0332aa3ce2c",
        "text": "Page: Message to Love. Sentence: The film unsparingly, often comically depicts the myriad problems associated with the famously chaotic festival—the main program of which was held on August 26–30, 1970—including gate-crashing, numerous crowd incursions onto the stage, Kris Kristofferson being booed offstage, and head promoter Rikki Farr's rants against the audience, which only intensified as the situation deteriorated: \"We put this festival on, you bastards, with a lot of love!\nPage: Kris Kristofferson. Sentence: Kristoffer Kristofferson (born June 22, 1936) is an American singer-songwriter, musician, and actor.\nPage: Kris Kristofferson. Sentence: He wrote and recorded the songs \"Me and Bobby McGee\", \"For the Good Times\", \"Sunday Mornin' Comin' Down\", and \"Help Me Make It Through the Night\".",
